    "faq": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How does a single-serve coffee maker work?",
            "text": "<p>A single-serve coffee maker brews one cup at a time by piercing a pre-filled pod and forcing hot water through the coffee grounds inside, sending fresh coffee straight into your mug. Because each cup is brewed on demand, there's no waiting on a full pot and no leftover coffee going to waste.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What kinds of pods can I use?",
            "text": "<p>Many single-serve machines are designed to accept popular single-cup pod styles, such as the common K-Cup style sold by lots of different coffee brands. It's always smart to check your machine's instructions for the pod size and shape it supports, since not every pod format fits every brewer.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can I use my own coffee instead of buying pods?",
            "text": "<p>Yes, many single-serve brewers either include or are compatible with a reusable refillable pod that you fill with your own ground coffee. This lets you choose any roast or blend you like, often saves money over pre-filled pods, and reduces single-use waste. A medium grind, similar to what you'd use for drip coffee, usually works best.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can I choose different cup sizes?",
            "text": "<p>Most single-serve machines let you select from a few brew sizes, so you can make anything from a small, strong cup to a larger, milder travel mug. Keep in mind that the same pod brewed at a larger size produces a more diluted cup, so smaller sizes generally taste bolder.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I clean and descale my single-serve coffee maker?",
            "text": "<p>Regularly wash the removable parts like the drip tray and pod holder, and run a descaling cycle every few months to remove mineral buildup from the water that can affect taste and performance. You can use a store-bought descaling solution or a diluted white vinegar rinse, always followed by a few cycles of fresh water. Descaling more often helps if you have hard water.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Are coffee pods recyclable, and what about the waste?",
            "text": "<p>It depends on the pod's materials and your local recycling rules; some plastic or aluminum pods can be recycled if you separate the lid, empty the grounds, and follow your area's guidelines, and certain brands offer their own take-back programs. If reducing waste is a priority, a reusable refillable pod is the simplest way to cut down on single-use packaging.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can a single-serve machine make a full pot or carafe?",
            "text": "<p>Single-serve machines are built to brew one cup at a time rather than a full carafe, which is part of what makes them so compact and quick. If you regularly need to serve several people at once, a traditional drip coffee maker or a hybrid machine that offers both single-cup and carafe brewing may suit you better.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
